  Good question — and it ties directly into what we just found. Let me split it into "can the loop
  be built" (mechanism) vs. "will the loop reach the goal" (outcome), because they have very
  different answers.

  Mechanism — very probable

  The pattern you read about is real and Claude Code actually supports it directly (there's a  /loop command — self-paced or on an interval — plus an autonomous-loop mode). The core idea is
  simple:  
  loop:
    1. propose a change (feature, model config, data)
    2. apply it
    3. run a command that emits a success metric
    4. compare to best-so-far → keep or revert, log it
    5. goal met OR out of budget? → stop, else repeat

  Your project is unusually well-suited to this because step 3 already exists:
  walk_forward_validate prints a mean AUC. So the "did it get better?" signal is automatable and
  cheap. That's the single most important prerequisite, and you have it. Mechanically, I'd put
  this at high feasibility — it's a weekend's worth of scaffolding.

  Outcome — much less certain, and not for engineering reasons

  Here's the honest part. A loop optimizes; it doesn't create signal that isn't in the data. We
  just measured that single-stock 5-day direction from technicals sits at ~chance (AAPL/TSLA ≈
  0.49) while indices carry real signal (VOO ≈ 0.59). So:

  - Goal "get VOO/SPY or a pooled model to ~0.60" → moderate-to-good chance a loop helps.
  - Goal "get TSLA daily to AUC 0.65" → low. The loop would churn forever, because the target may
  simply be unattainable from this data. "Until goal reached" silently becomes "until you run out
  of budget."

  So the probability isn't a property of the loop — it's a property of whether the goal is
  achievable. Pick an attainable, measurable goal and it works; pick an aspirational one and it
  spins.

  Three caveats that specifically bite ML loops

  1. The metric must be honest first. If you point the loop at a leaky AUC, it will happily chase
  inflated numbers and "succeed" at nothing real. This is exactly why Gap A (the embargo) matters
  before you automate — an optimizer will exploit leakage faster than a human. Fix the metric,
  then optimize it.
  2. Overfitting the validation set. A loop that maximizes walk-forward AUC will eventually p-hack  it — tweaking until it fits that particular validation noise. You need a held-out set of
  tickers/dates the loop never sees, checked only at the end. Otherwise the loop's "0.58" won't
  survive contact with new data.
  3. Guardrails. Hard max-iterations, "stop if no improvement in N rounds," and a cost ceiling —
  otherwise it runs indefinitely on an unsolvable goal.

  How I'd actually achieve it here

  1. Lock the goal as a number + a holdout, e.g. "mean embargoed walk-forward AUC ≥ 0.55 on a
  held-out ticker basket."
  2. Fix Gap A so the optimized metric is trustworthy.
  3. One entry-point script that prints the metric (you're ~90% there).
  4. A driver loop that each round proposes one change, runs the script, keeps/reverts on the
  holdout-safe metric, and logs every trial.
  5. Stop conditions as above.

  The realistic framing: this is less "set it and a working trading model pops out" and more "an
  automated experiment-runner that explores feature/model/config space faster than you'd do by
  hand, on top of honest metrics." With that framing it's genuinely useful; as a magic goal-seeker  it'll disappoint.

